  .kd-vianav-all:hover { color: var(--candle); border-bottom-color: var(--candle); }

  /* ── LEAD register ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Every hairline below is a border this layout already carried; not one was added to have
     something to draw. The real border stays for no-JS and reduced-motion visitors, and only
     under the motion gate is it handed to the pseudo-rule registers.css draws — which is why
     the colour has to be restated as `--rule-ink` here: that file cannot know which side or
     which ink any given hairline uses, and guessing would repaint the page's borders.

     All of these are TOP-anchored, `--rule-inset`'s default, and that is the whole reason this
     set and not a longer one. The trigger fires on an element's top edge while the rule is drawn
     where its border is, so a rule on a tall block's bottom edge draws a third of a screen below
     the line that fired it — off-screen every time. `.kd-tour-row`'s `border-bottom` is exactly
     that case and is deliberately left alone.

     Ink and lead share a node here (the one pair the language allows): the rule leads, the ink
     follows 0.18s behind, one causal gesture. `.kd-tour-list` is the exception and takes lead
     alone — it holds no copy, only the rule its rows hang from. */
  /* `:not(.is-bis)` is load-bearing. The encore's hairline is DASHED — a notation saying this
     work stands outside the programme — and the pseudo-rule is a solid 1px background, so
     handing that border over would quietly promote the bis row to an ordinary one for every
     visitor with motion on. It keeps its real dashed border and takes ink alone; the pseudo is
     suppressed with the others below. A dashed rule is not a ruled line anyway, and building a
     `repeating-linear-gradient` rule so one row can animate would be inventing machinery to
     animate something the design never draws as a line. */
  html.voct-motion .kd-program-item.reveal-rule:not(.is-bis),
  html.voct-motion .kd-cite.reveal-rule,
  html.voct-motion .kd-voces-note.reveal-rule {
    border-top-color: transparent;
    --rule-ink: var(--line, rgba(22,21,20,.16));
  }
  /* The act divider is the programme's strong rule; it keeps its own weight. */
  html.voct-motion .kd-movement.reveal-rule {
    border-top-color: transparent;
    --rule-ink: var(--line-strong, rgba(22,21,20,.34));
  }
  /* Night bands — the hairline is paper on dark, not ink on parchment. */
  html.voct-motion .kd-tour-list.reveal-rule,
  html.voct-motion .kd-tabula-list.reveal-rule,
  html.voct-motion .kd-run-head.reveal-rule,
  html.voct-motion .kd-gallery-foot.reveal-rule {
    border-top-color: transparent;
    --rule-ink: rgba(244,241,233,.14);
  }

  /* Where the layout DROPS a hairline, the pseudo-rule has to go with it, or the register draws
     a line the design decided not to print — the showreel move this whole register exists to make
     unnecessary. Four cases, all of them stated a few rules above as `border-top: 0`: an act
     opening the programme or following the dark interlude (the beat already gave the break), and
     a work following an act header or a bare clasp label (whose own rule IS the seam).
     `content: none` suppresses the pseudo-element outright rather than hiding it.

     NOTE for whoever edits those four rules: the register audit does not verify sibling
     combinators — `collect.mjs` keeps no sibling order — so nothing in the toolchain will notice
     if this list and the `border-top: 0` list above stop agreeing. They have to be changed
     together, by hand. */
  html.voct-motion :is(.kd-movement, .kd-clasp:not(.is-sung)) + .kd-program-item.reveal-rule::before,
  html.voct-motion .kd-movement.reveal-rule:first-child::before,
  html.voct-motion .kd-interlude + .kd-movement.reveal-rule::before,
  html.voct-motion .kd-program-item.is-bis.reveal-rule::before {
    content: none;
  }

